isks. Like other peripheral areas of Bertoua, it suffers from weak security force presence, degraded infrastructure, and proximity to illegal mining zones. Nighttime crime is a major concern.	Access via poor-quality unpaved roads. Motorcycle taxis are the only regular means of transport. Isolation intensifies after nightfall due to security concerns.	Informal settlements with customary land tenure. Housing consists of sun-dried bricks or basic concrete blocks. Real estate values are very low. Investment risk is high due to insecurity, uncertainty over land titles, and potential environmental degradation linked to mining activities.	Low-income population including internal migrants, informal miners, and some refugees from the Central African Republic. A high proportion of youth engaged in informal economic activities.	Yebdi is a low-income peripheral neighborhood on the outskirts of Bertoua, characterized by extremely limited infrastructure and serious security challenges. Its proximity to illegal gold-mining areas attracts a transient population and contributes to high crime rates. It is one of Bertoua’s least secure neighborhoods.	["Minimal — informal small shops", "Motorcycle taxi stop"]	["No notable sites"]	["High crime rate (theft, assaults)", "Minimal security presence", "Proximity to illegal mining sites", "Severely degraded infrastructure", "Environmental degradation due to mining activities", "Drug-related activities"]
96	\N	Bamenda	Commercial Avenue	Risque élevé	L'avenue commerciale est le principal pôle commercial de Bamenda et une cible potentielle d'attentats à l'engin explosif improvisé (EEI) et d'activités séparatistes. La zone est très fréquentée aux heures d'ouverture des commerces et bénéficie d'une présence sécuritaire visible, mais elle n'est pas à l'abri de la violence. Des explosions d'EEI se sont déjà produites dans le centre-ville de Bamenda. Les commerces ferment leurs rideaux pendant les couvre-feux spontanés (« ghost towns »). Risque de petite délinquance et de vol à l'arraché dans les zones marchandes bondées.	["Marché central de Bamenda", "Banques (Afriland First Bank, SCB Cameroun, BICEC, Ecobank)", "Restaurants et commerces", "Hôpital régional de Bamenda (à proximité)", "Hôtel Ayaba (à proximité)", "Mosquée centrale (à proximité)", "Stade de Bamenda (à proximité)"]	Route secondaire à sens unique d'environ 1,27 km. Situation centrale offrant une bonne connectivité avec les autres quartiers de Bamenda. Embouteillages fréquents aux heures d'ouverture des commerces. Les transports sont perturbés pendant les couvre-feux spontanés (« ghost towns »).	Emplacement immobilier commercial privilégié. Les biens situés le long de l'avenue commerciale comptent parmi les plus chers de Bamenda pour un usage commercial ou de détail. Le conflit a fait baisser les valeurs immobilières et réduit l'activité commerciale. De nombreuses entreprises ont fermé ou réduit leurs activités en raison de la situation sécuritaire.	Population mixte composée de commerçants, d'entrepreneurs, de fonctionnaires et d'ouvriers. Composition ethnique diversifiée, reflétant le rôle de Bamenda comme pôle régional. La densité de population est élevée aux heures d'ouverture des commerces.	["Marché principal de Bamenda", "Rond-point de l'hôpital", "Rond-point de City Chemist", "Hôtel Ayaba", "Mosquée centrale"]	["Cible d'attaque par des IED (centre urbain)", "Zones de marchés bondés sujettes à la petite délinquance", "Couvre-feux généralisés perturbant les activités commerciales", "Risque d'engagement croisé lors d'opérations sécuritaires", "Extorsion aux barrages routiers dans les zones environnantes"]	L'avenue commerciale est l'artère commerciale principale de Bamenda, une rue à sens unique d'environ 1,27 km bordée de banques, de boutiques, de restaurants et du marché principal de Bamenda. Son origine remonte à la période coloniale allemande, où elle servait de carrefour commercial, puis elle fut développée sous l'administration britannique. Cette artère constitue le cœur économique de la ville et de la région du Nord-Ouest, bien que la crise anglophone ait fortement réduit l'activité commerciale.	2026-06-22 23:29:27.83154-05	2026-06-22 23:29:27.83154-05	Commercial Avenue is Bamenda's main commercial hub and a potential target for improvised explosive device (IED) attacks and separatist activities. The area is heavily frequented during business hours and benefits from visible security presence, yet remains vulnerable to violence. IED explosions have already occurred in Bamenda’s city center. Businesses shutter their roll-down gates during spontaneous curfews ('ghost towns'). Risk of petty crime and snatch theft in crowded market areas.	One-way secondary road approximately 1.27 km long. Centrally located, offering good connectivity with other neighborhoods in Bamenda. Frequent traffic congestion during business hours. Transport is disrupted during spontaneous curfews ('ghost towns').	Prime commercial real estate location. Properties along Commercial Avenue rank among the most expensive in Bamenda for commercial or retail use. The conflict has depressed property values and reduced commercial activity. Many businesses have closed or scaled back operations due to the security situation.	Mixed population comprising traders, entrepreneurs, civil servants, and laborers. Ethnically diverse, reflecting Bamenda’s role as a regional hub. Population density is high during business hours.	Commercial Avenue is Bamenda’s principal commercial artery—a one-way street approximately 1.27 km long lined with banks, shops, restaurants, and Bamenda’s main market. Its origins date back to the German colonial era, when it served as a commercial crossroads, and it was further developed under British administration. This thoroughfare constitutes the economic heart of both the city and the Northwest Region, although the Anglophone crisis has severely curtailed commercial activity.	["Bamenda Central Market", "Banks (Afriland First Bank, SCB Cameroon, BICEC, Ecobank)", "Restaurants and shops", "Bamenda Regional Hospital (nearby)", "Ayaba Hotel (nearby)", "Central Mosque (nearby)", "Bamenda Stadium (nearby)"]	["Bamenda Main Market", "Hospital Roundabout", "City Chemist Roundabout", "Ayaba Hotel", "Central Mosque"]	["Target of IED attacks (urban center)", "Crowded market areas prone to petty crime", "Widespread curfews disrupting commercial activities", "Risk of crossfire during security operations", "Extortion at roadblocks in surrounding areas"]
