ally experienced armed robberies targeting student residences. A police station was established in Molyko following the 2003 student protests but was relocated in 2018 to upgraded facilities near the Bilingual High School of Buea. Students are advised to travel in groups and remain vigilant at night. Buea’s outskirts remain unstable, with frequent spontaneous curfews (‘ghost towns’) and broader security risks linked to the Anglophone crisis.	Centrally located in Buea with good road connectivity. Molyko Avenue (D61) and Malingo Street are the main thoroughfares. Traffic density remains high—even at night (until 2 a.m. on weekends). Transport is disrupted during spontaneous curfews (‘ghost towns’).	Land in Molyko ranks among the most expensive in Buea, and available plots are increasingly scarce. It is the preferred choice for investors seeking rental income (student housing, short-term rentals). Most students live off-campus in unregulated private accommodations, leading to arbitrary rent hikes. The university operates a Housing Office to assist students in finding safe accommodation.	Predominantly composed of students (~19,800) and young adults. A mix of students from across Cameroon, local residents, and traders. Vibrant student culture and active nightlife.	Molyko is Buea’s commercial and social hub, hosting the main campus of the University of Buea (founded in 1993, Cameroon’s flagship English-language university with approximately 19,800 students). The neighborhood bustles with students, shops, restaurants, and service providers. It features a lively nightlife, including nightclubs, bars, and restaurants. Land here is among the most expensive in Buea, making it the top choice for investors targeting rental income from student housing and short-term rentals.	["Main Campus of the University of Buea (~19,800 students)", "On-campus student housing (3,500 beds at Molyko and Bomaka residences)", "Nightclubs and entertainment venues (Las Vegas, Sainte-Claire, Malingo J