ns de XAF, les appartements d’une chambre environ 30 millions de XAF et ceux de deux chambres environ 48 millions de XAF. Les loyers mensuels varient de 90 000 XAF (studio) à 230 000 XAF (deux chambres). La zone devrait connaître une hausse annuelle des prix de 7 à 14 %, liée au développement du réseau BRT. Toutefois, la profondeur du bassin locatif y est moindre que dans des quartiers comme Odza ou Essos, et les zones périphériques, moins bien desservies par les routes, présentent un risque plus élevé de vacance locative.	Population majoritairement autochtone Beti/Ewondo (~60 %), avec un caractère cosmopolite hérité de son histoire, incluant les deux anciens établissements d’Ahala I et Ahala II.	["Carrefour Ahala Barrière", "Centre d'Ahala", "Échangeurs 1er et 2e d'Ahala", "Société industrielle de Mbang (SIM)", "Eco Park, attraction touristique"]	["demande locative plus faible", "risque de vacance dans les zones mal desservies", "criminalité urbaine classique"]	Ahala est un quartier résidentiel situé au sud de Yaoundé, le long de l’ancienne route Yaoundé-Kribi, sur une interfluve entre les affluents des rivières Esob Minlung et Biyeme. Initialement composé de deux petits établissements (Ahala I et Ahala II), il s’est développé en un quartier résidentiel de milieu de gamme doté d’immeubles modernes et d’établissements éducatifs. Son nom signifie « installe-toi précisément en ce lieu », en référence à la victoire de la tribu Edzoa sur la tribu Elom. Entouré par les quartiers de Nomayos, Afanayo, Mvan et Simbock, Ahala constitue un corridor résidentiel en pleine expansion, qui bénéficiera prochainement de la ligne BRT TransYaoundé.	2026-06-22 23:29:27.83154-05	2026-06-22 23:29:27.83154-05	Ahala is a residential neighborhood with moderate crime levels, typical of mid-range areas in Yaoundé. Local security is ensured by Police Station No. 19 (Ahala). Standard urban precautions apply, especially after nightfall.	Ahala lies along the former Yaoundé–Kribi road and is served by shared taxis and motorbike taxis (mototaxis). The Ahala 1st and 2nd interchanges connect the neighborhood to the broader road network. The planned TransYaoundé BRT corridor will link Oleumbe to Ahala, potentially reducing travel times.	Ahala is a residential neighborhood featuring modern buildings (R+1 and R+3 projects). Studio apartments cost approximately 18 million XAF; one-bedroom units, around 30 million XAF; and two-bedroom units, about 48 million XAF. Monthly rents range from 90,000 XAF (studio) to 230,000 XAF (two bedrooms). Prices are projected to rise annually by 7–14%, driven by the BRT network’s development. However, the rental market depth here is shallower than in neighborhoods like Odza or Essos, and peripheral zones—less well-connected by roads—carry a higher risk of rental vacancy.	Predominantly indigenous Beti/Ewondo population (~60%), with a cosmopolitan character rooted in its history, including the two former settlements of Ahala I and Ahala II.	Ahala is a residential neighborhood located south of Yaoundé, along the former Yaoundé–Kribi road, on an interfluve between the Esob Minlung and Biyeme rivers’ tributaries. Originally comprising two small settlements (Ahala I and Ahala II), it has grown into a mid-range residential area with modern buildings and educational institutions. Its name means 'settle precisely here', referencing the Edzoa tribe’s victory over the Elom tribe. Bordered by Nomayos, Afanayo, Mvan, and Simbock, Ahala forms an expanding residential corridor soon to be served by the TransYaoundé BRT line.	[{"name": "Ahala High School", "type": "school", "description": "Public secondary school serving the neighborhood."}, {"name": "Honoris Causa Bilingual School Group", "type": "school", "description": "Private bilingual school complex."}, {"name": "Diderot College", "type": "school", "description": "Private secondary school."}, {"name": "Higher Institute of Health Professions (ISPS)", "type": "school", "description": "Higher educ